  <article class="post">
  <h2 id="fourth-milestone-sky-reads-bluesky">Fourth milestone: Sky reads Bluesky<span class="soon">done</span></h2>
  <p class="meta"><time class="date" datetime="2026-10-25T16:57:29-04:00">2026-10-25</time></p>
  <p>Can Cairn hold a feed that never ends? Sky is the reading half of a Bluesky client: sign in
    with an app password, keep the session, and read the home feed however long it gets. It was the
    first of four milestones on Bluesky, which needs nearly everything left after Pairs: long
    lists, authentication, paging and requests that race. It is done: Sky is a signed app, built
    only from registry packages, and every path through it runs against a fake server with no
    account and no network.</p>
  <ol class="trail">
    <li>Scrolling in Cairn: the runtime owns every scroll offset and clamps it after every step,
      and a virtual list lays out only the rows on screen. A step costs about 0.17 ms at 1,000
      rows and at 100,000.</li>
    <li>Secrets: the session's tokens are captured from the server's replies into the Keychain by
      the host and never reach Lean, and the password is typed into a secure field that shows
      bullets, keeps no undo and copies nothing.</li>
    <li><code>cairn_atproto</code>, a package: XRPC, the session calls and the timeline, with
      proofs of one refresh at a time when a token expires and each call sent at most twice.</li>
    <li>Proofs about the feed: no duplicates and no gaps, a late page for an old cursor never
      changes it, and a refresh racing a load-more ends as one of the two would in order.</li>
    <li>Everything the host is trusted with, from the Keychain to what VoiceOver sees of a
      scrolled list or a secure field, is an assumption with a fuzz check of its own.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Sky lives in its own repository, <a href="https://github.com/iamnbutler/sky">iamnbutler/sky</a>,
    as the package <code>sky</code>, with its <a href="/packages/sky/claims/">claims on the
    registry</a>. Flinging through 1,102 posts with images, its Lean thread takes a median of 1.5
    to 1.7 ms a frame. Next is the acting half: likes, reposts and follows.</p>
  <ul id="log-m4" class="trail log"><li class="meta">Loading…</li></ul>
  </article>

  <article class="post">
  <h2 id="third-milestone-pairs">Third milestone: Pairs<span class="soon">done</span></h2>
  <p class="meta"><time class="date" datetime="2026-10-24T20:43:00-04:00">2026-10-24</time></p>
  <p>Can an app be built from packages alone, with images and requests it doesn't control? Pairs
    is a memory game: a grid of face-down cards, flip two, keep them if they match. Decks are theme
    packs, a JSON manifest and a set of images, from a folder or a URL. It is small enough to
    finish, and it needs what the Bluesky client after it will: images, JSON and many requests in
    flight at once.</p>
  <ol class="trail">
    <li>Images in Cairn: an image node whose size Lean knows before the pixels arrive, and an
      effect that loads one. The host decodes and paints; Lean never holds pixels.</li>
    <li>JSON as its own package, <code>cairn_json</code>, with a proof that decoding what
      was encoded gives it back.</li>
    <li>Requests in flight: at most 6 at once, and switching packs cancels the rest. A late reply
      for an old pack never changes the board.</li>
    <li>A grid that follows the window, proved for every width: no two cards overlap and every
      card is inside it.</li>
    <li>Proofs about the game: the deal has every face exactly twice, at most two unmatched cards
      are face up, and every game can be won.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Pairs lives in its own repository and is built only through the package path:
    <code>cairn new pairs --bin</code>, <code>cairn add</code> and <code>cairn build</code>.</p>
  <ul id="log-m3" class="trail log"><li class="meta">Loading…</li></ul>
  </article>

  <article class="post">
  <h2 id="second-milestone-yap-on-cairn">Second milestone: Yap on Cairn<span class="soon">done</span></h2>
  <p class="meta"><time class="date" datetime="2026-10-23T21:04:46-04:00">2026-10-23</time></p>
  <p>Can claims hold up on real software someone uses every day? Yap is a small dictation app for
    the Mac: hold a key, talk, and the transcript is typed into the app in front. Almost all of it
    lives outside its window, so Cairn has to learn the outside world first.</p>
  <ol class="trail">
    <li>Effects: the clock and timers, requests the host performs and answers, and outside inputs
      like a global key and the microphone. Each one is a recorded assumption, fuzzed.</li>
    <li>New surfaces: a native menu bar menu built from Lean, and an overlay that never takes focus.</li>
    <li>Proofs about dictation: a shortcut never sends, a transcript is saved before it is typed,
      a late reply never types, every recording ends.</li>
    <li>Yap rebuilt on Cairn, signed and installed, and used for a real day of work.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Yap lives in its own repository now, as the package <code>yap</code>, and depends on a tagged release of Cairn the
    way any app would. Getting it there made Cairn a package, with a <code>cairn</code> command
    and claim ids scoped by package: Yap's are <code>yap^N</code>.</p>
  <ul id="log-m2" class="trail log"><li class="meta">Loading…</li></ul>
  </article>

  <article class="post">
  <h2 id="first-milestone-a-proved-todomvc">First milestone: a proved TodoMVC<span class="soon">done</span></h2>
  <p class="meta"><time class="date" datetime="2026-10-23T10:57:34-04:00">2026-10-23</time></p>
  <ol class="trail">
    <li>The full TodoMVC app on the Mac, including real text fields and input methods.</li>
    <li>Every action is a claim with an id that the build refuses to let drift.</li>
    <li>Proofs: each action does what it says, the counts and filters stay right, and every
      control is present exactly when it should be and can actually be pressed.</li>
    <li>The real app is driven through a thousand generated sequences and must agree with the model.</li>
    <li>A map of the app, generated from the claims, showing the evidence behind each one.</li>
  </ol>
  <ul id="log-m1" class="trail log"><li class="meta">Loading…</li></ul>
  </article>
